A nested func whose hoisted name a *type* had already taken.

`renameNestedFuncs` composed `step` and `mark` into `step__mark` and checked it
against the funcs alone, so the `struct step__mark` beside it went unseen. Go's
package scope holds a type and a func together, and bubbletea emits a top-level
func for a func nested in a top-level func -- so `sngl generate` exited 0 and
wrote `type Step__mark` and `func Step__mark` into one file. `go build` said
`Step__mark redeclared in this block`; `format.Source` had nothing to say,
because a redeclaration parses.

The seed is now every kind sharing that namespace -- structs, enums, units,
components and funcs -- rather than funcs only, so the hoisted name takes the
suffix and the type keeps the spelling it was written under. A nested func in a
*component* body becomes a method and would not have collided; one nested in a
top-level func is the case, which is why `step` is at the root of the file here.

`mark` recurses so that passInlinePure leaves it alone, and `n` reaches it from
a handler so the call is not folded away -- either would delete the collision
before it could be emitted. Neither touches package-level state, which a
top-level func cannot read or write correctly yet (fix/toplevel-func-state-receiver).

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `(?m)^func Step__mark\(` -- the exported name belongs to the struct, so nothing emits a function under it

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

struct step__mark {
    a int
}

func step(a int) int {
    func mark(k int) int {
        if k <= 0 {
            return 0
        }
        return mark(k - 1) + k
    }
    return mark(a)
}

component main node {
    var n int = 0
    var keep step__mark = step__mark{a=1}

    text(value="{n}{keep.a}")
    button(text="go", @click {
        n = step(n) + keep.a
    })
}

window {
    main
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
